
Gave an old 2d inverse kinematics python function to Copilot for porting to C++. Several changes were suggested. A check was added to make sure target radius was not 0. A clamp function replaced a manual check that the arccos function domain was not exceeded. A law-of-cosines calculation replaced the previous trigonometric solution for angle calculation. An option was added to the function to return an error if the target exceeded the arm / leg length, or set the second bone to an angle of 0. Previously the function forced the limb to straighten without returning an error. A conversion from degrees to radians and back to degrees was added according to my preference to use degrees at the user level. The new changes were then ported to a C++ script to use for a character in OpenGL.

